==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|grc|ἀγωνιστικός}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/aɡəˈnɪstɪk/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of or relating to contests that were originally [[participated]] in by the Ancient Greeks; [[athletic]].<ref>{{w|Thomas Blount (lexicographer)|Thomas Blount}}, ''Glossographia,'' London: George Sawbridge, 1661: ''Agonistic(al):'' “warlike or skilful in exercises.”<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A28464.0001.001]</sup></ref>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1673|author={{w|Theophilus Gale}}|title=A Discourse of Christ’s Coming|location=London|publisher=John Hancock Senior and Junior|section=Chapter 2, Section 4, p. 70|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A41706.0001.001
|passage=These words, with what follow, are for the most part '''''agonistic''','' referring to the customes of the Grecian exercices, in their games. The words [translated] ''apprehended,'' signifie to ''catch,'' or apprehend the Crown, that hung over the Goal: so also [the word translated] ''perfect,'' is an '''Agonistic''' word that signifies the being ''crowned,'' or ''receiving the reward:''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|author={{w|Walt Whitman}}|chapter=A Song of Joys|title={{w|Leaves of Grass}}|location=London|publisher=David Bogue|page=147|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc2.ark:/13960/t12n4zs33&view=1up&seq=155&skin=2021&q1=agonistic
|passage=Joys of the solitary walk, the spirit bow’d yet proud, the suffering and the struggle?<br>The '''agonistic''' throes, the ecstasies, joys of the solemn musings day or night?}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|anthropology}} Characterised by [[conflict]] or [[hostility]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author={{w|Donald Symons}}|chapter=The Question of Function: Dominance and Play|editor=Euclid O. Smith|title=Social Play in Primates|location=New York|publisher=Academic Press|page=207|url=https://archive.org/details/socialplayinprim0000anim/page/207/mode/1up?q=agonistic
|passage=Although most '''agonistic''' interactions consist of signals of threat and submission, symbolic aggression is effective only because the possibility of escalation exists {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author={{w|Karen Armstrong}}|title=The Great Transformation|publisher=Atlantic Books|year_published=2007|page=66
|passage=This was clearly such an event, but like the ancient religion of Israel, it was also deeply '''agonistic'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Lucas Bessire|title=Behold the Black Caiman: A Chronicle of {{w|Ayoreo}} Life|publisher=The University of Chicago Press|section=Introduction, p. 8
|passage=Sociality swung wildly between extremes of collective affiliation and '''agonistic''' striving for dominance.}}
# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} [[argumentative|Argumentative]]; [[combative]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1992|author={{w|Kim Stanley Robinson}}|title={{w|Mars trilogy|Red Mars}}|location=New York|publisher=Bantam|year_published=1993|section=Part 1, p. 15|url=https://archive.org/details/redmars0000robi/page/15/mode/1up?q=agonistic
|passage=Sharply Frank said, “A place like this is a crimp in your plan, isn’t it?”<br>Boone’s gaze returned to him. The surrounding crowd slipped away, sensing the '''agonistic''' nature of the exchange.}}
# Struggling to achieve an [[effect]]; [[strained]] and [[contrived]].
# {{lb|en|pharmaceutical effect|biochemistry}} Pertaining to an [[agonist]].

